issue_context.body:
### Description

There is an unexpected change in marshalling behavior versus reference tests run on 3.1.10 and 4.0.30319.42000 which impacts both Marshal.StructureToPtr() and DllImport/PInvoke calls, breaking an interop scenario where we pass polymorphic parameters.

Given a class hierarchy where the base user class (above `object`) has no members, net50 appears to still allocate and pad storage for this base class despite having no members. Full test code attached, but simply:

```
class ClassA1
{
}

class ClassA2 : ClassA1
{
    public int F2 = 2;
}
```

In previous runtimes, an instance of `ClassA2` is marshalled as
`0x 02000000`

In net50 it is instead marshalled as
`0x 00000000 02000000`


### Configuration

This was observed on Windows in Microsoft.NETCore.App 5.0.1 in AnyCPU, x64, and x86 builds.

### Regression?

This behaves as expected in Microsoft.NETCore.App 3.1.10 and Net Framework 4.6.1 +4.0.30319.42000.

### Other information

Output from netcoreapp3.1 and net50 runs of the below sample program:

```
Environment.Version: 3.1.10

ClassA1: 00
ClassA2: 02000000

ClassB1: 01000000
ClassB2: 0100000002000000
```
```
Environment.Version: 5.0.1

ClassA1: 00
ClassA2: 0000000002000000

ClassB1: 01000000
ClassB2: 0100000002000000
```

```
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;

namespace MarshalTests
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Environment.Version: {0}", Environment.Version);
            Console.WriteLine();
            Console.WriteLine("ClassA1: {0}", MarshalToHexString(new ClassA1()));
            Console.WriteLine("ClassA2: {0}", MarshalToHexString(new ClassA2()));
            Console.WriteLine();
            Console.WriteLine("ClassB1: {0}", MarshalToHexString(new ClassB1()));
            Console.WriteLine("ClassB2: {0}", MarshalToHexString(new ClassB2()));
        }

        static string MarshalToHexString<T>(T item)
        {
            var size = Marshal.SizeOf<T>();
            var bytes = new byte[size];

            var pin = GCHandle.Alloc(bytes, GCHandleType.Pinned);
            try
            {
                Marshal.StructureToPtr<T>(item, pin.AddrOfPinnedObject(), fDeleteOld: false);
            }
            finally
            {
                pin.Free();
            }

            return string.Join("", bytes.Select(b => b.ToString("X2")));
        }
    }

    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
    class ClassA1
    {
        //public int F1 = 1;
    }

    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
    class ClassA2 : ClassA1
    {
        public int F2 = 2;
    }

    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
    class ClassB1
    {
        public int F1 = 1;
    }

    [StructLayout(LayoutKind.Sequential)]
    class ClassB2 : ClassB1
    {
        public int F2 = 2;
    }
}
```
